-import type { Finding } from "../finding.page-type.ts"
-
-export const theLiveOpsHookSetWasSplitByDateWhenTheFlatCopyWent = {
-  id: "01a069d1-0210-75ae-a586-009453195861",
-  pageTypeSlug: "finding",
-  slug: "the-live-ops-hook-set-was-split-by-date-when-the-flat-copy-went",
-  domainSlug: "page-type/agent-hook",
-  claim:
-    "Five of the eight agent settings files on this workstation name `tools/hooks/block-ops-cli.ts`, which this lane deleted at `93befef22f`. Three name the akasha copy instead. The split is by date and nothing else: every settings file written 2026-09-02 names the flat file, every one written 2026-09-03 names `akasha/hook-system/agent-hooks/block-ops-cli/block-ops-cli.agent-hook.code.ts`. No tracked file names the flat path at all, so the deletion was owed and the akasha copy is what a fresh spawn gets. What it costs is that five agents spawned before the cutover hold a hook command naming no file, so the ops CLI is unguarded for those five until each is spawned again.",
-  evidence:
-    "Measured 2026-09-03 at the console, over the eight `/tmp/agent-settings-*.json` files live at the time. Each was read for the two spellings and dated by its own mtime. Every file dated 2026-09-02 (09:27, 09:29, 10:36, 11:17, 21:15) names `tools/hooks/block-ops-cli.ts`; every file dated 2026-09-03 (12:30, 15:29, 17:51) names the akasha copy. No file names both and none names neither, so the two sets are exactly complementary and the cutover sits between 2026-09-02 21:15 and 2026-09-03 12:30.\n\nThe deletion was owed rather than merely cheap. A search of every tracked file for the substring `hooks/block-ops-cli` answers nothing outside `tools/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo`, an untracked build artifact that names all 33 deleted graph paths too, and the findings prose about the migration. The same search for `block-ops-cli` answers four files, all four under `akasha/hook-system/agent-hooks/`, so the search reaches the name and the zero on the flat path is a real zero rather than a dead instrument. `akasha/seat-system/agent-settings/pages/agents/agents.agent-settings.harness-settings.json`, which the spawn road reads, names neither path: the hooks are merged in from the akasha pages, which is why a fresh spawn takes the akasha copy with no file edit anywhere.\n\nThe akasha copy is the same rule and more of it. `block-ops-cli.agent-hook.code.ts:65-69` takes the head word of a segment and compares its basename to `ops`, which is what the flat file did at its lines 50-58. Beyond that it steps over a prefix that only runs the call behind it, it reads a name set before the call as not the call, and it prints its own scope. Its page carries nine invariants and there is a test beside it. The flat file carried none of the three.\n\nWhat this does not say. It says nothing about whether those five agents are alive now, only that their settings files were on disk when this was read; a settings file outlives the agent it was written for. It also says nothing about the spawns that call `ops` as argv rather than as a line of shell, which neither copy of the hook ever reached — that is already on file at `ops-has-no-unit-of-its-own-and-a-running-service-spawns-it-anyway`.",
-} as const satisfies Finding
